WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.440
Here's an odd thing about this experiment.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.400
When you mix an acid and a base

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.120
in a foam cup, the thermometer doesn't show

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.680
the highest temperature the mixture could reach.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.880
Heat starts escaping the moment it's made, so

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.040
by the time the reading peaks, some is

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:15.920
already gone.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.880
That's partly why the last video's answer fell

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.480
short of minus fifty seven.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.960
So how do you set it up, and

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.960
how do you measure a temperature you never

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.080
actually see?

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.180
Here's the set-up.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.700
Fifty millilitres of one mole per litre hydrochloric

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.300
acid goes into a polystyrene foam cup, standing

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.460
in a beaker so it can't tip.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.780
Foam is full of trapped air, so it

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.060
lets very little heat through: it's an insulator.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.300
A lid cuts the heat carried away by

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.060
warm air and vapour.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.020
Measure both solutions with measuring cylinders, because the

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.340
moles and the mass both come from those

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.140
volumes.

00:00:54.367 --> 00:00:57.886
Before mixing, measure the temperature of each solution,

00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:00.206
and wait until both are steady.

00:01:00.287 --> 00:01:02.286
If the acid reads twenty one point four

00:01:02.607 --> 00:01:05.006
and the base twenty one point six, use

00:01:05.047 --> 00:01:07.806
the average, twenty one point five, because you're

00:01:07.927 --> 00:01:09.646
mixing equal volumes.

00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:11.166
Why bother?

00:01:11.247 --> 00:01:13.486
A warm base would raise the temperature even

00:01:13.607 --> 00:01:15.006
with no reaction at all.

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:19.113
Now pour in the sodium hydroxide quickly, put

00:01:19.154 --> 00:01:20.553
the lid on, and stir.

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:23.833
Read the temperature every thirty seconds, and keep

00:01:23.914 --> 00:01:26.553
going for several minutes past the peak.

00:01:26.594 --> 00:01:29.273
It's tempting to stop at the highest reading.

00:01:29.314 --> 00:01:31.193
But the readings after the peak turn out

00:01:31.274 --> 00:01:32.873
to be the most useful part.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.180
Plot temperature against time.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.900
The temperature shoots up for about a minute,

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.540
peaks at twenty seven point eight, then falls

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.700
slowly in a straight line as the cup

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.060
loses heat.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.740
But the cup was losing heat during the

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.180
rise as well.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.340
So that peak is lower than the temperature

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.500
the mixture would have reached if no heat

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:53.300
had escaped.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.580
So draw a straight line through the cooling

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.740
points, and extend it back to the moment

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.620
of mixing.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:00.500
Why there?

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.940
The reaction itself is almost instant, so with

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.340
no heat lost, the temperature would jump to

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.380
its peak the moment you mix.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.740
Here the line meets twenty eight point one

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.700
degrees, our best estimate of the temperature with

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:14.980
no heat lost.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.620
Extending a line past your measured points is

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.060
called extrapolation.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.460
It assumes the cup was losing heat at

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.700
about the same rate during the rise as

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:24.500
afterwards.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.340
Now the calculation, as in the last video.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.540
The rise is twenty eight point one minus

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.860
twenty one point five, six point six degrees.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.540
With one hundred grams of solution, q is

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.020
about two thousand seven hundred and fifty nine

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.220
joules.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.260
There's zero point zero five zero zero moles

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:47.460
of water, so delta H is minus fifty

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.380
five kilojoules per mole.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.580
Using the highest reading instead gives only minus

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:53.620
fifty three.

00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:56.433
So what are the variables?

00:02:56.914 --> 00:03:00.113
Here you aren't changing anything between runs: you're

00:03:00.194 --> 00:03:02.833
measuring one value, three times.

00:03:02.914 --> 00:03:05.873
So the controlled variables matter most: the same

00:03:05.954 --> 00:03:09.473
concentrations and volumes, the same starting temperature, and

00:03:09.514 --> 00:03:12.593
the same cup, lid, stirring and timing.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.520
Why is minus fifty five still short of

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.200
minus fifty seven?

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.680
Follow each error through the chain: what it

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.560
does to delta T, then q, then delta

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.040
H.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.000
Heat escaping through the walls and lid, or

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.560
soaking into the cup and probe, makes delta

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.520
T too small, so q is too small,

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.680
and delta H comes out less negative.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.480
Most errors here push the same way, but

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.880
not every one: if you used only the

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.760
cooler acid's starting temperature, delta T would come

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.960
out too big, and delta H too negative.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.360
And never write "human error".

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.120
Name the actual cause.

00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:54.293
Now the three words from the equilibrium practical.

00:03:54.614 --> 00:03:57.733
Validity: does the experiment measure what it's meant

00:03:57.854 --> 00:03:58.053
to?

00:03:58.614 --> 00:04:01.013
Here, the rise must come from the reaction

00:04:01.254 --> 00:04:04.133
alone, with the heat kept in: both starting

00:04:04.214 --> 00:04:08.213
temperatures averaged, a lidded foam cup, and extrapolation.

00:04:08.694 --> 00:04:11.493
Reliability: do repeats agree?

00:04:11.734 --> 00:04:14.453
Three runs gave minus fifty five, minus fifty

00:04:14.694 --> 00:04:18.293
four and minus fifty six, so they're reliable.

00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:21.093
Accuracy: how close is it to the accepted

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:23.653
value, about minus fifty seven?

00:04:23.814 --> 00:04:26.773
Repeats can't fix that: heat escapes in every

00:04:27.014 --> 00:04:29.653
run, so every run comes out low.

00:04:29.814 --> 00:04:31.253
Cutting heat loss can.

00:04:32.467 --> 00:04:34.866
Before you start, the risk assessment.

00:04:35.507 --> 00:04:39.266
One mole per litre sodium hydroxide is corrosive:

00:04:39.347 --> 00:04:41.666
it can cause severe skin burns and eye

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:42.626
damage.

00:04:42.707 --> 00:04:45.506
So wear safety glasses or goggles, and rinse

00:04:45.587 --> 00:04:48.226
any splash off with plenty of water.

00:04:48.267 --> 00:04:50.546
The hydrochloric acid is low hazard at this

00:04:50.627 --> 00:04:52.706
strength, but keep it out of your eyes

00:04:52.787 --> 00:04:53.666
too.

00:04:53.827 --> 00:04:55.266
Stand the cup in a beaker so it

00:04:55.347 --> 00:04:58.386
can't tip, and stir with the stirrer, not

00:04:58.467 --> 00:04:59.586
a glass thermometer.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.480
Six words to keep: lid on, log on,

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:05.360
line back.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.040
A lid keeps more heat in, logging past

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.440
the peak gives you the cooling line, and

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.240
drawing that line back finds the temperature you

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.120
never saw.

00:05:16.334 --> 00:05:17.293
Your turn.

00:05:17.614 --> 00:05:20.413
Fifty millilitres each of one mole per litre

00:05:20.494 --> 00:05:24.093
nitric acid and potassium hydroxide, both at twenty

00:05:24.254 --> 00:05:26.733
point nine degrees, are mixed in a lidded

00:05:26.814 --> 00:05:28.013
polystyrene cup.

00:05:28.574 --> 00:05:30.093
The graph shows the readings.

00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:33.213
Use it to find the temperature change, then

00:05:33.454 --> 00:05:36.453
calculate the molar enthalpy of neutralisation.

00:05:36.734 --> 00:05:38.173
Pause here to answer.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.400
Draw the cooling line back to time zero:

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.280
it meets twenty seven point four degrees, so

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.400
delta T is six point five, and q

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:00.600
is two thousand seven hundred and seventeen joules.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.560
Divide by zero point zero five zero zero

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.680
moles of water: delta H is minus fifty

00:06:06.744 --> 00:06:08.560
four kilojoules per mole.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:11.640
Using the highest reading gives minus fifty two,

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:13.720
which ignores the heat already lost.

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:15.493
One more.

00:06:16.134 --> 00:06:19.093
Another student mixes the same hydrochloric acid and

00:06:19.174 --> 00:06:23.173
sodium hydroxide in an uncovered glass beaker, records

00:06:23.254 --> 00:06:25.733
the highest temperature on a thermometer, and gets

00:06:25.814 --> 00:06:28.773
minus forty six kilojoules per mole.

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:31.653
Identify the main source of error, explain its

00:06:31.814 --> 00:06:35.093
effect on the result, and suggest one improvement.

00:06:35.254 --> 00:06:36.453
Pause here to answer.

00:06:46.867 --> 00:06:48.786
The main error is heat loss.

00:06:49.347 --> 00:06:51.666
Glass lets heat through far more easily than

00:06:51.747 --> 00:06:54.786
foam, the beaker soaks up some heat itself,

00:06:54.827 --> 00:06:57.386
and the open top lets warm air escape.

00:06:57.667 --> 00:07:00.466
So the measured rise is too small, q

00:07:00.547 --> 00:07:03.106
is too small, and delta H is less

00:07:03.187 --> 00:07:03.746
negative.

00:07:04.307 --> 00:07:06.386
Improve it with a lidded foam cup, and

00:07:06.467 --> 00:07:08.626
extrapolate the cooling line back.

00:07:08.947 --> 00:07:12.146
Writing "human error" doesn't name a cause, so

00:07:12.254 --> 00:07:13.346
nobody can fix it.

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:17.926
So: insulate and cover the cup, measure and

00:07:18.007 --> 00:07:21.686
average both starting temperatures, log past the peak,

00:07:21.727 --> 00:07:23.846
and extrapolate the cooling line back to the

00:07:23.927 --> 00:07:25.366
moment of mixing.

00:07:25.447 --> 00:07:28.486
Then trace each error through delta T, q

00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:30.006
and delta H.

00:07:30.567 --> 00:07:33.126
Next: where neutralisation solves real

00:07:33.767 --> 00:07:33.926
problems.

00:07:35.134 --> 00:07:36.573
Here are the key notes.

00:07:36.814 --> 00:07:38.733
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:38.814 --> 00:07:39.613
to copy them.
